{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The ledger of qualified semantic claims over object identity, distinct from the publication ledger and the immediate semantic source of an entity cut.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1045,
          "quote": "- the attribute ledger records qualified semantic claims over object identity\n- the publication ledger records immutable published cuts of domain artifacts\n  and object projections",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FPublication%20Law",
          "start_line": 1043
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1053,
          "quote": "The attribute ledger is the immediate semantic source of an entity cut.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FAttribute%20Ledger%20Law",
          "start_line": 1053
        }
      ],
      "key": "attribute_ledger",
      "label": "Attribute ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Git's publication-ledger role records immutable published cuts of domain artifacts and object projections. Filesystem and Git suffice for the initial publication lane; a later database complements this as a query plane.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1039,
          "quote": "`Git is the publication ledger. A database is the query plane.`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FPublication%20Law",
          "start_line": 1039
        }
      ],
      "key": "publication_ledger",
      "label": "Publication ledger"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A published semantic unit recovered by a builder from authoritative source surfaces and boundaries already committed to by the operating model, then qualified into published truth.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1079,
          "quote": "Entities are recovered, not invented. A builder project constructs an entity\ncut by trawling authoritative source surfaces for the boundaries the operating\nmodel already commits to, then qualifying them into published truth.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FEntity%20Cut%20Construction%20Law",
          "start_line": 1077
        }
      ],
      "key": "entity_cut",
      "label": "Entity cut"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [
        "The builder operates in a declared representation space in which identity is a geometric direction."
      ],
      "definition": "A representation-substrate specialization of entity-cut construction, built by a directional interventional procedure and stored as an immutable package attached to its entity cut. The representation may be learned, an embedding over ledger claims, or an equivalent metric space.",
      "exceptions": [
        "Not required for ordinary institutional recovery."
      ],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1169,
          "quote": "This law is the representation-substrate specialization of *Entity Cut\nConstruction Law*. It applies only where the builder operates over a declared\nrepresentation space in which identity is a geometric direction — a learned\nrepresentation, an embedding over ledger claims, or an equivalent metric\nspace. It is not required for ordinary institutional recovery.\n\nWhere it applies, a builder project constructs the Markov-object overlay by a\ndirectional, interventional procedure. The framework below is both how the\noverlay is *built* and the minimum structure that must be *stored* when it is\npublished.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMarkov%20Object%20Construction%20Law",
          "start_line": 1160
        }
      ],
      "key": "markov_overlay",
      "label": "Markov-object overlay"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A geometric combination over attribute columns derived as a low-rank projection of paired evidence. Its canonical mean-offset form across contexts is d(object) = μ(evidence_object) − μ(evidence_null).",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1195,
          "quote": "Derive the identity axis as a low-rank projection over the paired\nevidence. The canonical form is the mean offset:\n\n`d(object) = μ(evidence_object) − μ(evidence_null)`",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMarkov%20Object%20Construction%20Law%2F2.%20Identity-Direction%20Extraction",
          "start_line": 1192
        },
        {
          "end_line": 1207,
          "quote": "The identity direction is not an attribute column. It is a geometric\ncombination over columns.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMarkov%20Object%20Construction%20Law%2F2.%20Identity-Direction%20Extraction",
          "start_line": 1206
        }
      ],
      "key": "identity_direction",
      "label": "Identity direction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The source-defined assessment of whether direction d in representation space R preserves declared identity under a selected held-out treatment and required observation rule. Its result domain distinguishes satisfaction, falsification, indeterminacy with actual performance, invalid_basis, and out_of_frame; overlay status is a separate downstream status.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1236,
          "quote": "The verification assessment binds \"direction d in representation space R\npreserves the declared identity under the selected held-out treatment and\nrequired observation rule.\" Supported preservation satisfies it; an observed\nidentity counterexample falsifies it. An unrun or inconclusive treatment leaves\nit indeterminate with actual performance retained; an unusable representation,\nfixture or interpretation is invalid_basis, and an established assessment\nscope/capability shortfall is out_of_frame. The recorded candidate/accepted\noverlay status follows this assessment and the other publication obligations;\nit is not itself a verdict and does not decide the stronger statistical claim.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMarkov%20Object%20Construction%20Law%2F3.%20Verification%20By%20Treatment",
          "start_line": 1228
        }
      ],
      "key": "treatment_assessment",
      "label": "Verification by treatment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The published world-model semantic substrate, with domain artifacts, entity cuts, treatments, covariance edges, adjoint mappings, and applicable temporal reference artifacts. Projections, query, traversal and executable delivery remain downstream.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1379,
          "quote": "The semantic kernel remains primary. All world-model projections should remain\ntraceable to their governing objects, evidence, treatments, covariance edges,\nadjoint mappings and applicable temporal reference artifacts. Projection is\ndownstream convenience, not semantic authority.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FProjection%20Law",
          "start_line": 1376
        }
      ],
      "key": "world_model_kernel",
      "label": "World-model semantic kernel"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
