    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["markov_overlay", "treatment_assessment"],
      "conditions": ["Assessing a candidate identity direction for overlay publication."],
      "exceptions": ["The underlying entity cut remains lawful and publishable without the overlay."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1226,
          "quote": "A candidate identity direction is accepted only if projection along\nit preserves object identity under held-out treatments. The builder\nmust record at least one acceptable verification:\n\n- α-sweep: apply `x − α · d` on held-out contexts, show expected\n  identity shift and its saturation behaviour\n- transplant: apply `x + α · d` to null-peer instances, show\n  identity acquisition\n- boundary test: overwrite attributes outside the projection support\n  and show identity survives (this is what discriminates geometric\n  blanket from set-membership blanket)\n\nA direction that has no acceptable verification is a candidate, not an\naccepted overlay. Publishing an unverified direction is a violation of\nthe Representation Law. The underlying entity cut remains lawful and\npublishable without the overlay.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMarkov%20Object%20Construction%20Law%2F3.%20Verification%20By%20Treatment",
          "start_line": 1211
        }
      ],
      "key": "overlay_treatment_gate",
      "statement": "Acceptance requires identity preservation under held-out treatment and at least one recorded acceptable verification: α-sweep x − α·d showing expected identity shift and saturation; transplant x + α·d to null peers showing identity acquisition; or overwriting attributes outside projection support showing identity survives. No acceptable verification leaves a candidate; publishing an unverified direction violates the source's Representation Law. Supported preservation satisfies, a counterexample falsifies, unrun/inconclusive treatment is indeterminate with performance retained, unusable representation/fixture/interpretation is invalid_basis, and established scope/capability shortfall is out_of_frame. Candidate/accepted status follows assessment plus other publication obligations, is not itself a verdict and does not establish formal Markov-blanket closure.",
      "uncertainty": "Representation Law and Epistemic Status Of The Construct are referenced but not supplied."
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["markov_overlay"],
      "conditions": ["The candidate appears in multiple contexts."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line": 1257,
          "quote": "For candidates that appear in multiple contexts, decompose the\nattribute evidence into:\n\n- **invariant core**: attributes whose contribution to the identity\n  direction is stable across ≥ N of N contexts\n- **context coat**: attributes selected by the specific context,\n  carrying contextual reinterpretation rather than identity per se\n\nBoth are evidence. The core carries identity. The coat carries\ntreatment. The coat/core ratio is a characterization, not a quality\nmetric.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation/codex/5a5b5ad80f0ba4b56b83aadd42ac130de53ba803/source/standards/WORLD_MODEL_METHOD.md#World%20Model%20Method%2FMarkov%20Object%20Construction%20Law%2F4.%20Core%20And%20Coat%20Decomposition",
          "start_line": 1247
        }
      ],
      "key": "core_coat_decomposition",
      "statement": "Decompose evidence into invariant core—attributes with identity-direction contribution stable across ≥ N of N contexts—and context coat—context-selected attributes carrying contextual reinterpretation. Both are evidence; core carries identity, coat carries treatment, and coat/core ratio characterizes rather than grades quality.",
      "uncertainty": "N and the operational criterion for stable contribution are not specified here."
